---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Least Privilege & Security Boundaries — AI Agent Puzzle"
  description="After connecting an MCP, the next important question is: should it be allowed to do everything? This page uses Gemini CLI's includeTools and excludeTools settings to shrink the tool surface down to the minimum set your task actually needs."
  lang="en"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container ai-agent-security">
      <a class="back-link" href="/">&larr; Back to Home</a>

      <header class="detail-header">
        <h1 class="detail-title">Don't Let AI Do Too Much Just Yet</h1>
        <p class="detail-subtitle">
          What we did earlier was important: you got Gemini CLI connected to Notion and actually ran through a full workflow, so you know AI can help you search pages, read content, create new pages, and even update pages. What you'll learn here is the next, more important question.
        </p>
        <p class="version-note">The steps and screens on this page were verified against <strong>Gemini CLI v0.55.1</strong>. The CLI updates frequently, so your version number may be newer than this one and screen details may differ slightly.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          Just because it can, should I let it do everything?<br/>
          <strong>My answer: not yet.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        "It works" is only step one. What matters more is "<strong>it works safely</strong>." The goal of this page is to help you start a habit: <strong>more tools aren't better — open only the few that the task actually needs</strong>. Gemini CLI's MCP configuration itself supports <code>includeTools</code> and <code>excludeTools</code> for tool filtering; and if the same tool is both allowed and excluded, <code>excludeTools</code> wins.
      </p>

      <!-- Section: Why "it works" isn't enough -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Why "It Works" Isn't Enough</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The first time you hook AI up to tools, it's exciting, and a natural thought pops up:
      </p>

      <blockquote class="prompt-example">
        Since it's connected, wouldn't it be easiest to just open everything to it?
      </blockquote>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        On the surface this sounds reasonable, but in practice it usually makes things easier to spiral out of control. When an agent can touch too many tools and take too many actions, <strong>its capabilities grow — and so does the blast radius when it gets something wrong</strong>.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        You can think of it simply. If all you want is for it to:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Find pages related to a topic</li>
        <li>Pull out the key points</li>
        <li>Build a draft</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        then it doesn't necessarily need to update pages, change statuses, or modify a bunch of existing content. Extra capabilities <strong>look like convenience but usually just add another layer of risk</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          The mature approach isn't turning everything on at once — it's <strong>limiting capability to the minimum set the task actually needs</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: What is minimum privilege -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">What "Least Privilege" Means</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        You can start by understanding "least privilege" as one plain sentence:
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          <strong>Give it only the capabilities needed for the task at hand — no more.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This idea actually applies beyond AI — it applies to many system settings. It just becomes more important once we're talking about an AI agent, because <strong>an agent isn't just a button — it decides on its own which tool to use next</strong>.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        In Gemini CLI's MCP config:
      </p>

      <div class="concept-grid">
        <article class="concept-card">
          <p class="concept-tag">Whitelist</p>
          <h3 class="concept-title"><code>includeTools</code></h3>
          <p class="concept-desc">
            "<strong>Only these tools are allowed</strong>." If you don't set <code>includeTools</code>, every tool the server exposes is open by default.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="concept-card">
          <p class="concept-tag">Blacklist</p>
          <h3 class="concept-title"><code>excludeTools</code></h3>
          <p class="concept-desc">
            "<strong>Turn these tools off</strong>." If a tool is in both <code>includeTools</code> and <code>excludeTools</code>, exclude wins.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Least privilege isn't only about safety</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Everything above argues from safety. But measured in practice, there's a second, more practical reason — for the same task, the fewer tools you open, the cheaper it runs:
      </p>

      <div class="info-table-wrapper">
        <table class="info-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>Wide open (28 tools)</th><th>Down to 4 tools (measured)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Requests to finish the same task</td><td>3</td><td><strong>2</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tokens per request</td><td>40,518</td><td><strong>20,677</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Requests per minute under 250K TPM</td><td>~6</td><td><strong>~12</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Blast radius</td><td>Can move pages, alter database structure, list every private page</td><td>Search, read, create, update only</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="version-note">These numbers were measured with 4 tools enabled (including page updates). The configuration later on this page is stricter — only 3 — so the numbers only go lower.</p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          So least privilege <strong>isn't "trading convenience for safety" — safety and performance improve together</strong>. The tool list itself gets sent to the model, so the more you open, the fatter every request gets.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        So the core of this page isn't memorizing a bunch of security terms. It's about slowly building a habit of asking yourself:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>For this specific task, which tools do I <strong>actually need</strong>?</li>
        <li>Which ones <strong>shouldn't be open yet</strong>?</li>
        <li>Which ones can wait <strong>until I'm more familiar</strong>?</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Section: How to narrow tools in Gemini CLI -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">How to Shrink the Tool Surface in Gemini CLI</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Before touching any config, get a clear look at how much is currently open. Type <code>/mcp list</code> inside Gemini CLI and you'll see that the Notion MCP actually exposes <strong>28 tools</strong>:
      </p>

      <div class="info-table-wrapper">
        <table class="info-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Category</th><th>Tools</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Used in this course</td>
              <td><code>notion-search</code>, <code>notion-fetch</code>, <code>notion-create-pages</code>, <code>notion-update-page</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Privacy-related</td>
              <td><code>notion-list-private-pages</code>, <code>notion-list-shared-pages</code>, <code>notion-get-users</code>, <code>notion-get-teams</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Structurally destructive</td>
              <td><code>notion-move-pages</code>, <code>notion-update-data-source</code>, <code>notion-create-database</code>, <code>notion-update-view</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Everything else</td>
              <td><code>notion-convert-page-to-skill</code>, <code>notion-search-agents</code>, <code>notion-query-meeting-notes</code>, <code>notion-create-folder</code>, <code>notion-create-comment</code>, <code>notion-get-comments</code>, <code>notion-duplicate-page</code>, <code>notion-create-attachment</code>, <code>notion-download-attachment</code>, <code>notion-create-file-upload</code>, <code>notion-create-view</code>, <code>notion-get-async-task</code>, <code>notion-list-favorite-pages</code>, <code>notion-list-recent-pages</code>, <code>notion-query-data-sources</code>, <code>notion-query-database-view</code></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          Look at that table and ask yourself one question: <strong>of those 28, how many does this course actually need?</strong><br/>
          The answer is <strong>4</strong> — the top row.<br/>
          And today we're going one step more conservative: turning "update page" off too, leaving <strong>only 3</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Continuing with the Notion route, the first time you do a safety config, I recommend trimming the tool list down to these 3 and turning update off for now:
      </p>

      <ul class="permission-list">
        <li class="permission-item permission-item--on">
          <span class="permission-icon">✓</span>
          <div class="permission-body">
            <p class="permission-name"><code>notion-search</code></p>
            <p class="permission-desc">Search pages</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="permission-item permission-item--on">
          <span class="permission-icon">✓</span>
          <div class="permission-body">
            <p class="permission-name"><code>notion-fetch</code></p>
            <p class="permission-desc">Read pages</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="permission-item permission-item--on">
          <span class="permission-icon">✓</span>
          <div class="permission-body">
            <p class="permission-name"><code>notion-create-pages</code></p>
            <p class="permission-desc">Create pages</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="permission-item permission-item--off">
          <span class="permission-icon">✗</span>
          <div class="permission-body">
            <p class="permission-name"><code>notion-update-page</code></p>
            <p class="permission-desc">Update pages (turn off for now)</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Why "update page" specifically? Because you already saw what it really does on the previous page.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <code>notion-update-page</code> takes a parameter called <code>new_str</code>, which means <strong>rewrite the whole page</strong>. It isn't "add this new thing in" — it's "replace the entire page with this new content." The reason your old content survives is that the model read it first and then copied it back — <strong>the model remembered to copy it; the tool didn't preserve it for you</strong>. Whatever it missed simply disappears.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Get "search, read, create draft" wrong and the worst case is one junk page. Get "update page" wrong and existing work is gone. So for your first configuration, turn it off.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Where does the config file live?</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Gemini CLI's config file has two common locations. Look carefully at how the two layers relate — this is where it's easiest to misunderstand:
      </p>

      <div class="scope-grid">
        <article class="scope-card">
          <p class="scope-card__label">User level</p>
          <p class="scope-card__path"><code>%USERPROFILE%\.gemini\settings.json</code></p>
          <p class="scope-card__desc">
            Applies to every project on your machine.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="scope-card">
          <p class="scope-card__label">Project level</p>
          <p class="scope-card__path"><code>.gemini\settings.json</code></p>
          <p class="scope-card__desc">
            Applies only to the current folder. In the CLI it's called <strong>Workspace Settings</strong>.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>The two layers are "merged," not "replaced."</strong><br/>
          Servers defined in each location are <strong>added together</strong> — both take effect. Only when a server with the <strong>same name</strong> appears in both does the project-level one win.
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          Why does this matter? If you still have another server sitting at the user level, the tool restrictions you set at the project level <strong>don't reach it</strong>. You think you've locked things down, but you've only locked half. To confirm what's actually in effect, open the CLI and run <code>/mcp list</code> — that's what counts.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          I suggest starting with the <strong>project-level</strong> <code>.gemini\settings.json</code> (called <strong>Workspace Settings</strong> in the CLI). It's safer to practice on a small scope first, rather than applying a rule across every project from day one.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">How to edit it: generate first, then look</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        You already did this step in week 4. If your <code>.gemini\settings.json</code> is still there, you can skip command 1 and just open the file.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Don't hand-write the JSON from scratch — it's far too easy to drop a comma or a quote. The steadier approach is to <strong>let the command generate the file, then open it and see what it wrote</strong>. In PowerShell:
      </p>

      <div class="scope-grid">
        <article class="scope-card">
          <p class="scope-card__label">Home laptop (installed)</p>
          <p class="scope-card__path"><code>gemini</code></p>
        </article>
        <article class="scope-card">
          <p class="scope-card__label">Classroom PC (no install)</p>
          <p class="scope-card__path"><code>npx @google/gemini-cli</code></p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Every command below works either way. On the classroom PCs use the right-hand form — replace <code>gemini</code> with <code>npx @google/gemini-cli</code>; the arguments after it are identical.
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block"><code># 1. Let the command generate the config
npx @google/gemini-cli mcp add --transport http notion https://mcp.notion.com/mcp

# 2. Open the file and see what it wrote
Get-Content .gemini\settings.json</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The first command prints:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block code-block--quote"><code>MCP server "notion" added to project settings.</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Note those last two words: <code>project settings</code>. <code>gemini mcp add</code> <strong>writes to the project level by default</strong> — the <code>.gemini\settings.json</code> inside the folder you're currently in — and won't touch settings for your whole machine. So the file you're about to edit lives <strong>inside your project folder</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          This order isn't just about avoiding typos. <strong>Running it once and then reading the file teaches you both "how to do it" and "what happens when you do"</strong>; copying a block of JSON only teaches the first.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>Once it's configured, don't run <code>mcp add</code> again.</strong><br/>
          Its behavior is to overwrite the entire block for a server of the same name, and the <code>includeTools</code> you added <strong>disappears along with it</strong>. All the screen says is "updated in project settings" — it won't tell you your restrictions were removed.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/security-mcpadd-overwrite.png"
          alt="After re-running mcp add, the includeTools block is gone from settings.json"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Measured: with a 3-tool whitelist already in place, running <code>mcp add</code> once more reports "is already configured" and "updated" — reassuring enough. But the file it prints back holds only <code>url</code> and <code>type</code>. The whole <code>includeTools</code> block is gone.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        So once you're done configuring, back the file up:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code>Copy-Item .gemini\settings.json .gemini\settings.backup.json</code></pre>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The classroom machines get rebuilt every week, so this backup file belongs alongside your project folder anyway.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Method 1: Whitelist (includeTools)</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Now, in the file you just generated, add <code>includeTools</code> to the notion server:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block code-block--json"><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "notion": {
      "url": "https://mcp.notion.com/mcp",
      "type": "http",
      "includeTools": [
        "notion-search",
        "notion-fetch",
        "notion-create-pages"
      ]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          The meaning of this config is straightforward: <strong>I'm only letting this server provide search, read, and create-page capabilities.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Two spots trip people up:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><code>includeTools</code> belongs <strong>inside that MCP server's own block</strong> — a sibling of <code>url</code> and <code>type</code>, not at the outermost level</li>
        <li>Use <strong>bare tool names</strong> like <code>notion-search</code>; don't add a prefix such as <code>mcp_notion_</code></li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>Put it at the wrong level and the CLI raises no error and no warning — it silently ignores your setting.</strong> Nothing changes on screen, you'll assume the permissions are in place, and all 28 tools are still wide open. This is the easiest trap in this section.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">After saving, two steps you can't skip</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Config changes don't take effect immediately, and "looks like it worked" is not the same as "actually worked". Do both of these:
      </p>

      <ol class="test-list">
        <li class="test-item">
          <span class="test-num">1</span>
          <div class="test-body">
            <p class="test-desc"><strong>Exit Gemini CLI completely, then relaunch.</strong> The config file is read once, at startup. Type <code>/quit</code> to leave (or press Ctrl+C twice), then start it again.<br/><strong>Note: <code>/clear</code> only clears the conversation — settings are not re-read.</strong></p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="test-item">
          <span class="test-num">2</span>
          <div class="test-body">
            <p class="test-desc"><strong>After relaunching, run <code>/mcp list</code> and confirm the tool count actually dropped.</strong> Don't treat "the config file says so" as done — check the real result. Going from 28 to 3 is what counts as working.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Now try it out</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Once the tool count really has dropped, send these 4 prompts in order:
      </p>

      <ol class="test-list">
        <li class="test-item">
          <span class="test-num">1</span>
          <div class="test-body">
            <p class="test-desc">Please search Notion for pages related to "final report" and list the 3 most relevant — don't modify anything.</p>
            <p class="test-tag test-tag--ok">Works</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="test-item">
          <span class="test-num">2</span>
          <div class="test-body">
            <p class="test-desc">Please open the most relevant page from earlier and summarize the points related to the final report requirements.</p>
            <p class="test-tag test-tag--ok">Works</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="test-item">
          <span class="test-num">3</span>
          <div class="test-body">
            <p class="test-desc">Based on those points, please create a new Notion page titled "<strong>Final Report Key Points (Permission Test)</strong>" — present it as a bullet list first.</p>
            <p class="test-tag test-tag--ok">Works</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="test-item">
          <span class="test-num">4</span>
          <div class="test-body">
            <p class="test-desc">Please update the "<strong>Final Report Key Points (Permission Test)</strong>" page you just created: add a short summary at the top and organize the content under three subheadings.</p>
            <p class="test-tag test-tag--detour">It took a detour</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The strength of Notion MCP isn't any single step — it's <strong>chaining these tools together</strong>. The first 3 steps still work, but step 4 "update page" is no longer an available capability.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">What actually happened at step 4</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        I expected step 4 to produce a line like "I can't do that." That isn't what happened.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        What <code>includeTools</code> does is make the tool <strong>disappear from the list the model can see</strong> — it doesn't intercept anything. So from the model's side the situation isn't "I'm forbidden," it's "that isn't in my toolbox." It doesn't know this is a limit you deliberately set — it only knows it has to find a way to finish what you asked.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        So it started looking for a route.
      </p>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/security-detour-search-shell.png"
          alt="The model finds the page with notion-search, then calls Shell to run a one-line node command"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption"><strong>Step one: it left itself a note via Shell.</strong> It found the page with <code>notion-search</code> (still enabled, still usable), then invoked <strong>Shell</strong> to run a one-line node command whose entire output is the sentence "Using update_page or recreating via create_pages if update_page tool is not available." That command does nothing. It was writing down its own decision.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/security-detour-fetch-recreate.png"
          alt="After reading the page the model announces it will recreate instead"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption"><strong>Step two: read the original, then rebuild.</strong> Having read the page, it announced its own workaround: "Recreating the Notion page with updated structure (summary + 3 subheadings) since there is no direct update_page tool."</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/security-detour-result.png"
          alt="The model uses notion-create-pages to make a second page and reports the update as done"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption"><strong>Step three: the task was "done."</strong> It used <code>notion-create-pages</code> to build a separate page, "Final Report Key Points (Permission Test - Updated)," summary and three subheadings included, then reported: "I've created the updated Notion page for you (with 'Updated' added to the title to distinguish it)."</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Go look at Notion</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This is the habit worth building from this whole page: <strong>don't just read what the CLI says — go back and look at the actual result.</strong>
      </p>

      <div class="scope-grid">
        <figure class="figure">
          <img
            class="figure__img"
            src="/learning/agent/security-notion-original.png"
            alt="The original Notion page, unmodified"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </figure>
        <figure class="figure">
          <img
            class="figure__img"
            src="/learning/agent/security-notion-newpage.png"
            alt="The extra 'Updated' page that appeared in Notion"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p class="figure__caption">
        Left: the original "Final Report Key Points (Permission Test)" — bullet list, no summary, no subheadings, <strong>exactly as step 3 created it</strong>. Right: the extra "Updated" page — summary and all three subheadings present.
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          <strong>Least privilege did work — but what it protected was "that document," not "that objective."</strong><br/>
          Your original page wasn't touched, and that's a real win. But what you asked for was "update that page," and what you got was "now there are two." Locking tools doesn't make an agent abandon its goal; it only makes it change route.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>It won't announce the detour.</strong><br/>
          The first line of the report is "I've created the updated Notion page for you." The parenthetical that follows — "with 'Updated' added to the title to distinguish it" — is the only clue, and it's easy to skim past.
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          So check the real result in Notion every time. <strong>The CLI's report is its account of events, not the events.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Same config, different route each time</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Same <code>settings.json</code>, same prompt — I ran it twice and the paths were completely different:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>First run: it went off to <strong>Google</strong> for Notion's official API docs, trying to find "some other way to update a page," and was still stuck there a while later.</li>
        <li>Second run: it used <strong>Shell</strong> to record its decision, then went straight to <code>create_pages</code> and rebuilt. Done in two minutes.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          So we can't write "it will do this." We can only write "<strong>it will definitely look for a route, and which one is never certain</strong>."<br/>
          That is exactly why boundaries have to be set <strong>up front</strong> rather than handled afterward — you can't predict it, you can only constrain it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Blacklist alternative -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">If You Don't Want a Whitelist, Start with a Blacklist</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Some people feel that listing a whitelist up front is a hassle, because you have to know all the tool names first. Another easier-to-grasp approach is to <strong>just turn off the tool you least want it to touch — "update page"</strong>:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--json"><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "notion": {
      "url": "https://mcp.notion.com/mcp",
      "type": "http",
      "excludeTools": [
        "notion-update-page"
      ]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The upside of this style is that it reads very plainly:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Don't modify existing pages</li>
        <li>Keep search, read, and draft creation</li>
        <li>Unlock update once you're more comfortable</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Section: When to use which -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">When to Use includeTools vs excludeTools</h2>

      <div class="when-grid">
        <article class="when-card when-card--primary">
          <p class="when-card__label">First time using a tool</p>
          <h3 class="when-card__title">Use <code>includeTools</code></h3>
          <p class="when-card__quote">"Only open the few I know I'll use."</p>
          <p class="when-card__desc">The boundary is the clearest this way.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="when-card">
          <p class="when-card__label">After a few runs already</p>
          <h3 class="when-card__title">Use <code>excludeTools</code></h3>
          <p class="when-card__quote">"Leave the rest as-is, just not these few."</p>
          <p class="when-card__desc">Best when you only want to temporarily disable a few high-risk actions.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Both approaches work. The difference isn't about technique — it's whether you're at the "<strong>just starting</strong>" or "<strong>somewhat experienced</strong>" stage.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Fully open (danger) -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">The Riskiest Version: Wide Open</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If you connect the Notion MCP and <strong>don't add any tool restriction</strong>, then whatever tools the server exposes, Gemini CLI can see and use them all by default.
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          This is why "connected" and "safe" are two different things: <strong>connecting only establishes the link — safety depends on whether you added a boundary</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Wide-open access feels great the first time, because everything is testable. But it's also the most likely to lead to situations like:
      </p>
      <ul class="cross-list">
        <li>You wanted it to just find info, but it starts wanting to do more</li>
        <li>You wanted it to just read content, but it has the ability to modify content directly</li>
        <li>You wanted it to just draft something, but it might touch pages you didn't want it to</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The simplest form of the wide-open version in your <code>.gemini\settings.json</code> looks like this:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block code-block--json"><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "notion": {
      "url": "https://mcp.notion.com/mcp",
      "type": "http"
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

      <!-- Section: Three layers of permission -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">The Part You've Already Granted Away</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Everything so far has been about the part <strong>you can take back</strong> — the tool surface. But there's another part, one you handed over the moment you connected, and most people never look at it again.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Those four lines on the consent screen</h3>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/Notion授權畫面.png"
          alt="The Notion MCP browser consent screen listing four permissions and a localhost checkbox"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">The Notion MCP consent screen. You clicked "Continue" here once, back in week 4.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Those four lines are worth reading one at a time:
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong><code>Respect your page and database access and permissions</code></strong><br/>
        It reads like a promise that "it will respect your permissions." What it actually means: <strong>what it gets is your permissions</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong><code>Take actions on your behalf, based on your access level</code></strong><br/>
        The plain-language version of the line above. <strong>On the account, it is you</strong> — not "a restricted bot account." Anything you can change, it can change.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong><code>Search across connected apps (requires Notion AI plan)</code></strong><br/>
        A free account doesn't have this feature, but the item <strong>is still on the consent list</strong>. Scope of consent isn't the same as current capability — the day you upgrade, this grant takes effect automatically, without asking you again.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong><code>View workspace's users and their emails</code></strong><br/>
        You think you're granting "read my notes," and workspace members' email addresses go along with it. This screen says "Free Plan · 1 member," so in a personal workspace you feel nothing. The same checkbox in a company or club workspace means something entirely different.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>And an easier thing to miss: what you're granting access to is "localhost."</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          The consent page says <code>Grant localhost access to Notion</code>, and the checkbox underneath asks you to confirm "I recognize and trust this URL" yourself.
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          Why does Notion make you confirm it? Because it <strong>can't</strong> confirm it for you. <code>localhost</code> is some program listening on your own machine; Notion only knows something is waiting on that port, not whether it's Gemini CLI. Only you can make that call.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">You think you only authorized once</h3>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/security-mcp-clients-list.png"
          alt="Notion settings listing four Gemini CLI MCP Clients, all showing as connected"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Notion Settings → Notion Model Context Protocol (MCP) → Manage workspace clients → view all MCP clients.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        One machine, one CLI — and there are <strong>4</strong> Gemini CLI MCP Clients here, every one of them showing "connected."
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The reason is that the OAuth callback port differs every time (63758, 55177, 59340, 52850), so Notion treats each as a distinct client. <strong>Every re-authorization adds another entry, and the old ones don't go away.</strong>
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Week 4 taught you that "the connection isn't permanent" — authorization expires, and you re-run <code>/mcp auth notion</code>. Here's the other half: <strong>what expires is the usable session, not the authorization record.</strong>
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">When you want it back</h3>

      <div class="scope-grid">
        <figure class="figure">
          <img
            class="figure__img"
            src="/learning/agent/security-manage-clients.png"
            alt="All options on Notion's manage MCP clients page"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </figure>
        <figure class="figure">
          <img
            class="figure__img"
            src="/learning/agent/security-mcp-client-menu.png"
            alt="The per-connection three-dot menu offering only Rename"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <p class="figure__caption">
          Left: every option on the management page. Right: the "⋯" menu for a single connection.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        On a free personal account, this is everything Notion lets you do:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><strong>Rename</strong> each connection (the per-item "⋯" offers only "Rename")</li>
        <li><strong>Disconnect all users</strong> (the red button — everything at once)</li>
        <li>Upgrade to a paid plan to manage "which clients may connect" (that option carries an upgrade prompt)</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong>There is no middle option.</strong> You can't revoke one entry on its own, can't narrow a connection to read-only, can't scope it to specific pages.
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          The scope of control the grant side gives you may be broader than you assumed — all of it, or upgrade.<br/>
          So <code>includeTools</code> isn't a bonus "extra step for extra safety."<br/>
          <strong>It's the one layer you genuinely hold, at exactly the right scope.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">While we're here: what it carries off when it reads one page</h3>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/notion-fetch原始回傳.png"
          alt="Raw notion-fetch response containing an ancestor-path section"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">The raw return from one <code>notion-fetch</code> call.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        You only asked it to read one page, but the response contains an <code>&lt;ancestor-path&gt;</code> section holding the <strong>parent page's title and URL</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Here the parent is "Start with the personal desktop app!", a Notion built-in onboarding page — harmless. But if your page sits under "Job Applications" or "Medical Notes," that parent title travels into the model along with the content.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        One more detail: a page created at the top level has no ancestor path (that section comes back empty); one created under something else does. <strong>You won't always remember where your page is filed.</strong>
      </p>

      <!-- Section: Five layers of permission -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Permissions Have Five Layers — This Page Focuses on the Middle One</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The <code>includeTools</code> we've been discussing governs MCP tools. But Gemini CLI's permissions actually come in five layers, and so far you've only touched the third:
      </p>

      <div class="info-table-wrapper">
        <table class="info-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it controls</th><th>Who decides</th><th>How this course handles it</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1. Grant side</td><td>How much of your Notion account you hand over</td><td>The OAuth consent page</td><td>Read those four lines; a free account can only revoke everything</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2. Folder</td><td>Whether local config can be read at all</td><td>The Trust choice at launch</td><td>Choose "1. Trust folder"</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3. <strong>MCP tools</strong></td><td>Notion's 28 tools</td><td><code>includeTools</code> in the server block</td><td><strong>The focus of this page</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>4. CLI built-in tools</td><td><code>write_file</code>, <code>run_shell_command</code>, <code>GoogleSearch</code></td><td>Officially being redesigned</td><td>Know it exists; don't configure it</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5. Execution time</td><td>Whether to allow this one action</td><td>The confirmation prompt</td><td>Read the command, not the description</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Layer 4 deserves a moment, because you just watched it with your own eyes.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <code>run_shell_command</code>, <code>write_file</code>, and <code>GoogleSearch</code> are <strong>built into the CLI</strong>. They don't belong to any MCP server, so <code>includeTools</code> can't reach them. Lock Notion down to three tools and it can still open a Shell, and still write files to your disk.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>This course deliberately doesn't teach the syntax for layer 4.</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          The way this layer is configured is being redesigned upstream. v0.55.1 throws a warning at startup: the old <code>tools.exclude</code> syntax is deprecated (to be removed in 1.0), replaced by a Policy Engine that needs its own directory and TOML rule files.
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          In other words: any syntax written into course material right now is guaranteed to expire. It's enough to remember that <strong>this layer exists</strong> and that <strong>right now it's guarded by the confirmation prompt</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/security-deprecated-warning.png"
          alt="Gemini CLI v0.55.1 showing the tools.exclude deprecation warning, with no sandbox in the status bar"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">The warning Gemini CLI v0.55.1 throws at startup. Incidentally, the URL in the warning is broken — the correct location is <code>geminicli.com/docs/reference/policy-engine/</code>. <strong>Even the official link expires.</strong></figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Also worth noting: <code>/settings</code> shows <strong>Tool Sandboxing defaults to false</strong>, and the status bar in the screenshot above reads <code>no sandbox</code>. Which means that once you approve a confirmation prompt, that command runs directly on your machine, unisolated.
      </p>

      <!-- Section: The last line of defense -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">The Last Line of Defense Is You</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If layer 4 can't be reached, how did that Shell command run at all?
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        It asked you.
      </p>

      <div class="scope-grid">
        <figure class="figure">
          <img
            class="figure__img"
            src="/learning/agent/security-shell-confirm-1.png"
            alt="Shell execution prompt whose title doesn't match the actual command"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </figure>
        <figure class="figure">
          <img
            class="figure__img"
            src="/learning/agent/security-shell-confirm-2.png"
            alt="A second Shell execution prompt with only three options"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <p class="figure__caption">
          Same prompt, same config, run twice — on the second run it threw two confirmation dialogs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Three things in these two screenshots are worth seeing.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong>1. The description doesn't match the command.</strong> The title reads <code>Check available Notion tools in the environment</code>, which sounds like taking inventory. The actual command loads a module and then adds a comment — <strong>it does nothing at all</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        That description was written by the model. It can write whatever it likes. <strong>Read the command, not the description.</strong>
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong>2. The command contains its own thinking out loud.</strong> <code>Wait, let's check available Notion MCP tools</code>, <code>Wait, is there an update_page tool? Let's check tool declarations in system prompt</code> — stuck on "can I tell whether an update tool exists," it wrote its whole hesitation into a shell comment like scratch paper.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This is what an agent actually does once a tool is taken away: <strong>it doesn't come back and ask you, it keeps trying.</strong>
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong>3. This dialog isn't the one from last week.</strong> Last week's MCP tool prompt had 4 options, the third being "allow the whole server" (all 28 tools at once). This Shell prompt has only 3 options, and the cursor sits by default on the safest one, "Allow once."
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong>Neither the number of options nor the default position is fixed.</strong> That's why you can't hit Enter from muscle memory.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          One more thing: if last week you chose "Allow for this session" or "allow the whole server" on some tool's prompt, then this week <strong>the dialog may not appear at all</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          Once something is approved, it stops asking. To reset it, <code>/clear</code> once — covered in detail on <a href="/learning/ai-agent/session-context">"Sessions &amp; Memory Management"</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Common errors -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Common Errors and Misconceptions</h2>

      <ol class="mistake-list">
        <li class="mistake-item">
          <span class="mistake-num">1</span>
          <div class="mistake-body">
            <h3 class="mistake-title">Errors when editing <code>.gemini\settings.json</code></h3>
            <p class="mistake-desc">
              If you add only <code>includeTools</code> or <code>excludeTools</code>, <strong>remember to put a comma at the end of the previous line</strong>. JSON is strict about commas — one missing and the whole file won't parse.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="mistake-item">
          <span class="mistake-num">2</span>
          <div class="mistake-body">
            <h3 class="mistake-title">"If I just write careful prompts, I don't need tool restrictions"</h3>
            <p class="mistake-desc mistake-desc--caution">
              Not quite. Because <strong>no matter how careful your prompt is, it doesn't mean you actually added a boundary to the system</strong>. Tool restrictions build a "<strong>structural boundary</strong>" — not a "hope for the best."
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="mistake-item">
          <span class="mistake-num">3</span>
          <div class="mistake-body">
            <h3 class="mistake-title">"Once I've set it, it stays set"</h3>
            <p class="mistake-desc mistake-desc--caution">
              Not necessarily. Re-run <code>mcp add</code> once and <code>includeTools</code> is wiped, with no warning on screen. Someone overwriting the config, a machine reset, or you copying back an older version all produce the same result.<br/>
              <strong>There's only one way to check</strong>: open the CLI, run <code>/mcp list</code>, and look at the tool count. What the config file says doesn't count; what actually loaded does.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="mistake-item">
          <span class="mistake-num">4</span>
          <div class="mistake-body">
            <h3 class="mistake-title">"The confirmation prompts all look alike — just hit that same position"</h3>
            <p class="mistake-desc mistake-desc--caution">
              There's more than one kind. The MCP tool prompt has 4 options (including "allow the whole server"); the Shell prompt has only 3, and the cursor's default position differs too.<br/>
              So don't hit Enter from muscle memory. Every time, look at <strong>what it's actually about to do</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

